{"y":2003,"label":"2002–03","coach":"Jim Calhoun","conf":"Big East","confDiv":"East","confShort":"Big East","w":23,"l":10,"cw":10,"cl":6,"confFinish":"2nd in Big East","finish":"sweet16","seed":5,"region":"South","apPre":15,"apHigh":3,"apFinal":23,"srs":15.84,"sos":7.63,"ortg":108.0,"drtg":96.8,"ppg":79.5,"oppg":71.2,"story":{"headline":"Calhoun's Courage, Okafor's Rise","text":"On February 3, 2003, Jim Calhoun announced he had prostate cancer; he had surgery three days later and was back on the sideline against St. John's at Gampel Pavilion only 16 days after the operation. Around him a future champion was taking shape. Sophomore Emeka Okafor was the national and Big East Defensive Player of the Year and first-team All-Big East, and Ben Gordon made the second team. UConn (23–10, 10–6) shared the Big East East Division title, won at No. 9 Notre Dame 87–79, and beat Seton Hall and Syracuse at the Garden before Pittsburgh won the Big East final 74–56. As a No. 5 seed the Huskies beat BYU and Stanford, then lost a Sweet Sixteen shootout to top-seeded Texas, 82–78. Every starter would return.","moments":[{"date":"2003-02-22","title":"Calhoun returns","text":"Sixteen days after prostate cancer surgery, Jim Calhoun returns to the bench against St. John's at Gampel."},{"date":"2003-03-15","title":"Big East tournament Championship: L vs Pittsburgh","text":"UConn lost to Pittsburgh 56–74 (Championship) at Madison Square Garden, New York, NY."},{"date":"2003-03-20","title":"NCAA First Round: W vs BYU","text":"UConn beat BYU 58–53 in the NCAA tournament first round at Spokane Veterans Memorial Arena, Spokane, WA."},{"date":"2003-03-22","title":"NCAA Second Round: W vs Stanford","text":"UConn beat Stanford 85–74 in the NCAA tournament second round at Spokane Veterans Memorial Arena, Spokane, WA."},{"date":"2003-03-28","title":"NCAA Sweet Sixteen: L vs Texas","text":"UConn lost to Texas 78–82 in the NCAA tournament sweet sixteen at Alamodome, San Antonio, TX."}],"honors":["Big E